[38;2;124;124;124mexactly![0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, sin(α+β)/sin(α+γ) = 1?? That means sin(α+β) = sin(α+γ). With 2α+β+γ=π: α+β[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= π - α - γ. So sin(α+β) = sin(π-α-γ) = sin(α+γ). YES! Because α+β = π-(α+γ), so[0m[38;2;124;124;124msin(α+β)=sin(α+γ). [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the Menelaus product = sin(α+β)/sin(α+γ) = 1. So my computation was correct, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mand the product IS 1, using 2α+β+γ=π! Great, so Menelaus works.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo Lemma 1 proof via Menelaus:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Define P' on ray BC beyond C with ∠P'DC = φ = ∠DBC.[0m
